WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.581
Online evaluation on sampled traffic.

2
00:00:02.861 --> 00:00:07.192
Offline evals (Studio 3) tell you whether a change is safe to ship.

3
00:00:07.472 --> 00:00:12.869
Online evals tell you whether the shipped system is still good on the traffic it actually gets.

4
00:00:13.149 --> 00:00:15.475
The mechanics reuse what you already built.

5
00:00:15.755 --> 00:00:16.544
Sample.

6
00:00:16.824 --> 00:00:23.203
Take a uniform random sample of production traces for measurement, as Studio 3’s review-queue lesson showed.

7
00:00:23.483 --> 00:00:32.805
Keep it separate from targeted samples (thumbs-down, judge failures, latency outliers), which are for discovery and are deliberately enriched with failures.

8
00:00:33.085 --> 00:00:37.587
Hamel Husain and Shreya Shankar’s evals FAQ makes the same split.

9
00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:45.909
Run the graders asynchronously, off the request path, so evaluation never adds latency or failure modes to the user’s request.

10
00:00:46.189 --> 00:00:46.957
Grade.

11
00:00:47.237 --> 00:00:56.731
Run the cheap, certain graders on every sampled trace: code-based checks such as citation verification, schema rules, and forbidden-content filters.

12
00:00:57.011 --> 00:01:04.157
Run the LLM judge from Studio 3, with the prompt you calibrated against human labels, on the same sample.

13
00:01:04.437 --> 00:01:13.845
Several tracing tools (Langfuse and LangSmith among them) can run evaluators on production traces for you; the statistics are the same either way.

14
00:01:14.125 --> 00:01:15.256
Size the sample.

15
00:01:15.536 --> 00:01:16.560
You have the math.

16
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.824
To estimate a pass rate near 0.

17
00:01:19.104 --> 00:01:26.763
9 within about plus or minus 3 points at 95 percent confidence, the normal approximation gives n of about 1.

18
00:01:27.043 --> 00:01:28.941
96 squared times 0.

19
00:01:29.221 --> 00:01:30.480
9 times 0.

20
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.488
1, divided by 0.

21
00:01:32.768 --> 00:01:38.421
03 squared, which is about 384 judged traces per reporting window.

22
00:01:38.701 --> 00:01:43.779
At 20,000 requests a day, a 2 percent sample gives that daily.

23
00:01:44.059 --> 00:01:51.077
At 2,000 a day, report weekly instead of sampling more aggressively than your judge budget allows.

24
00:01:51.357 --> 00:01:55.560
The judge has a cost too: price it with the same table as everything else.

25
00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:56.608
Correct.

26
00:01:56.888 --> 00:02:00.109
A judge’s raw pass rate is biased by its own errors.

27
00:02:00.389 --> 00:02:17.371
Studio 3 showed the Rogan and Gladen correction: with the judge’s true positive rate (TPR) and true negative rate (TNR) measured on human labels, the estimated true pass rate is (observed + TNR - 1) / (TPR + TNR - 1).

28
00:02:17.651 --> 00:02:28.381
Chart the corrected rate with its interval, and keep a person relabeling a small random sample every week or two, because a judge’s TPR and TNR drift when traffic does.
